CORE
JmlSignalsTyped
--function "JmlSignalsTyped.doubleOrThrow:(I)I" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlSignalsTyped.java --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for typed `signals (T e) p;` JML clauses.

`signals (T e) p;` constrains methods that throw an exception
of (a subtype of) T: when the method exits with such an
exception in flight, p must hold, with `e` bound to the
inflight pointer cast to T.

Lowering pipeline:
  1. Parser captures clause.signal_type, clause.signal_var,
     and the predicate text (jml_parser.cpp).
  2. lower_jml_contracts pre-substitutes `e` with
     `(T*) @inflight_exception` BEFORE running
     resolve_jml_expr, so that calls like `e.getFoo()` see a
     properly-typed receiver and the trivial-getter rewrite
     can fire.
  3. At the function exit, before any signals_only block,
     the assertion
       (\inflight != null && classid(\inflight) ∈ T*) ⇒ p
     is inserted. signals_only's null-assignment is
     deliberately ordered AFTER this assertion so we still
     see the actual inflight value.
  4. ensures clauses are gated by `\inflight == null` so the
     normal-return postcondition no longer fires on
     exception-throwing paths (a pre-existing latent issue
     surfaced by combining ensures with signals).

The test method `doubleOrThrow(int)` returns `2 * x` for
`x ≤ 1000` and throws `BadInputException(x)` otherwise.
The full JML
  requires x >= 0;
  ensures \result == x * 2;
  signals_only BadInputException;
  signals (BadInputException e) e.getBadValue() == x;
exercises requires (precondition), ensures (gated by normal
return), signals_only (allowed exception type), and signals
(typed exception postcondition with a trivial-getter call on
the bound variable).
